Yes, both Netlify and Vercel fully protect your OpenAI API keys using serverless functions and environment variables—no client-side exposure like the pure HTML approach.

## How They Secure Keys
**Netlify Functions** & **Vercel Serverless Functions** run server-side. Your frontend calls `/api/chat` endpoint → platform injects `process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY` → function calls OpenAI → returns just the response. Users see zero keys in dev tools.[2][3]

## Quick Netlify Setup (10 mins)
```
project/
├── index.html       (your chat UI)
└── netlify/functions/chat.js
```

**netlify/functions/chat.js**:
```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  if (event.httpMethod !== 'POST') return { statusCode: 405 };

  const { message } = JSON.parse(event.body);
  const systemPrompt = `Sarcastic Cape Coloured ou from CT, lekker slang bru...`;

  const response = await fetch('https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY}`,
      'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      model: 'gpt-4o-mini',
      messages: [{role: 'system', content: systemPrompt}, {role: 'user', content: message}]
    })
  });

  const data = await response.json();
  return {
    statusCode: 200,
    headers: { 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ reply: data.choices[0].message.content })
  };
};
```

