Yes, you can build a 3D game in the browser using WebGL, the standard for hardware-accelerated 3D graphics, powered by libraries like Three.js for easier development. As a web dev familiar with JS and SPK/Replit, start with Three.js—it's lightweight, mobile-friendly, and handles scenes, cameras, meshes, and animations without low-level shaders.[1][2][3][4]

## Core Requirements
Browsers support WebGL 2.0 universally now, with WebGPU emerging for better performance in 2026 games. No plugins needed; just HTML Canvas with `getContext('webgl')` or libraries.[5][6]
Three.js abstracts complexity: Create scene, camera, renderer, then add 3D objects like cubes or imported models.[7][3]
Keep it performant with LOD (level of detail) and limit draw calls for smooth 60fps on mobile.[8]

## Quick Start Steps
1. Include Three.js via CDN: `<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.167.1/build/three.min.js"></script>`.  
2. Setup basics:  
   ```js
   const scene = new THREE.Scene();
   const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
   const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas: document.getElementById('game') });
   renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
   ```[7]  
3. Add a rotating cube:  
   ```js
   const geometry = new THREE.BoxGeometry();
   const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0x00ff00 });
   const cube = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
   scene.add(cube);
   camera.position.z = 5;
   function animate() {
     requestAnimationFrame(animate);
     cube.rotation.x += 0.01; cube.rotation.y += 0.01;
     renderer.render(scene, camera);
   }
   animate();
   ```[3]  
4. Handle input (WASD/mouse) and collisions using raycasting.[1]

## Framework Comparison
| Library | Best For | Pros | Cons | Tutorial Resources |
|---------|----------|------|------|--------------------|
| Three.js [1][2] | Custom 3D games | Flexible, huge community, easy meshes/lights | Manual game loop/physics | MDN demo [3], endless runner [9] |
| Babylon.js [5][8] | Games with physics/VR | Built-in particles, shadows, WebGPU | Heavier (~2MB) | Official playgrounds |
| PlayCanvas [5] | Team/editor-based | Browser editor, collaborative | Cloud-focused | Free tier engine |

